branches.task:
EVAL-METHODOLOGY REVIEW (round {args.round}).

Review these proposed selections for an IRIS analyst-agent eval:

{args.proposal}

Prior-round critique to check is addressed:
{args.prior_critique}

Judge ONLY through an eval-methodology lens: (1) Are the layer-3 dimensions (Sufficiency/Completeness/Insight) orthogonal, individually judge-able in isolation, and calibratable against humans? Any overlap/redundancy or a missing dimension? (2) Are the thresholds sound — is 'zero fabricated numbers' the right hard gate, is fact-coverage ≥0.8 defensible vs arbitrary, is 1% float tolerance right, is pairwise-advisory-until-κ≥0.7 correct? (3) Does the L1/L2 gate vs L3 advisory split hold up? For EACH selection output AGREE or OBJECT with a concrete fix. Be specific; cite the Anthropic/DL-0020 principles (isolated judge per dim, give-a-way-out, reference-anchored) where relevant.

IRIS-DOMAIN REVIEW (round {args.round}).

Review these proposed selections for an IRIS analyst-agent eval:

{args.proposal}

Prior-round critique to check is addressed:
{args.prior_critique}

Judge ONLY through the lens of REAL IRIS analyst answers (NL question -> SQL -> data -> findings). Ground your review in the concrete golden pair 'dau-basic' (Q: 'What is the daily active users for the last 7 days?'; expected_answer_facts: reports DAU counts / covers last 7 days / uses fct_daily_user_active). (1) Do Sufficiency/Completeness/Insight actually capture what makes an IRIS finding good-vs-bad for a real user? Anything an analyst answer needs that's missing (e.g. caveats on data gaps, correct metric interpretation)? (2) Are the L2 grounding thresholds realistic against real answers (do real good answers cite tables? will 'zero fabricated numbers' misfire on rounded/approximate phrasings)? (3) Is expected_answer_facts a strong enough reference anchor, and how to mark 'required' vs optional facts? Output AGREE or OBJECT per selection with a concrete fix.

SKEPTIC / RISK REVIEW (round {args.round}).

Stress-test these proposed selections for an IRIS analyst-agent eval:

{args.proposal}

Prior-round critique to check is addressed:
{args.prior_critique}

Adversarial lens only: (1) How could an optimizer GAME each threshold to raise the score without real improvement (Goodhart)? e.g. terse answers gaming Insight, omitting numbers to dodge the fabrication gate, restating expected_answer_facts verbatim to game fact-coverage. (2) Is the L2 anti-hallucination gate actually a sufficient trust boundary, or are there fabrication modes it misses (wrong-but-plausible numbers that match executed rows by coincidence, misattributed segments)? (3) Are any gates too strict (block valid answers) or too loose (pass bad ones)? (4) Does advisory-until-κ≥0.7 leave a window where bad L3 signal still influences ranking? Output AGREE or OBJECT per selection with the concrete failure mode + fix.

phases.task:
Three specialists reviewed the proposed IRIS eval selections (round {args.round}).

PROPOSAL UNDER REVIEW:
{args.proposal}

SPECIALIST REVIEWS:
{steps.panel.output}

Decide consensus. Rules: consensus=true ONLY if NO specialist raised a material/blocking objection (minor wording nits are fine). Fold every ACCEPTED fix into revised_selections; reject fixes that conflict with DL-0020 principles or are out of v1 scope, and say why in critique. Output ONLY JSON:
{
 "consensus": true|false,
 "revised_selections": "<the full, current best selections: layer-3 dimensions + per-layer thresholds, incorporating accepted fixes>",
 "changes_from_proposal": "<what changed vs the proposal, or 'none'>",
 "critique": "<remaining open objections that block consensus, or 'none'>"
}
